# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

This resource requires a token with enterprise owner permissions, and is only available on GitHub Enterprise Cloud.

~> Destroying this resource cancels the invitation while it is still `pending`, but **removes the user from the enterprise** once they have accepted it.

~> Creating this resource for someone who is already a pending invitee or an enterprise member adopts them rather than failing. Terraform then owns that membership, so a later destroy will remove them.

Specify exactly one of `user_id` (the numeric GitHub account ID) or `username`. Prefer `user_id`: Terraform resolves the current username by ID, so an account rename preserves the membership and an old username being reused by someone else does not change the managed account.

Existing username-based resources and `enterprise:username` imports learn their account ID during refresh. To migrate, replace `username` with that same `user_id`; no replacement is required. A username-only configuration must use the current login. Changing it to a different account replaces the membership. If the account was renamed before its ID was recorded, Terraform cannot recover its identity from the old login; configure the trusted `user_id` and import using `enterprise:current-username` if needed.

By default, Terraform sends a new invitation on the next apply when a previously managed invitation is no longer pending. Set `reinvite = false` to retain the resource without sending another invitation. In that state, `status` is `expired` and `invitation_id` is empty. GitHub does not distinguish an expired invitation from one cancelled outside Terraform through this API, so `expired` indicates that the previously managed invitation is no longer pending.

-> Enterprise Managed User (EMU) enterprises provision members through their identity provider and cannot invite users this way.

